---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { SLOP_FEED } from '../../slop';
import '../../post.css';
---

<Layout
	title="About Slop — Nathan Arthur"
	description="How the Slop posts on nathanarthur.com are made."
	ai
	feed={SLOP_FEED}
>
	<p class="mb-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-faint">
		<a href="/slop">&larr; Slop</a>
	</p>

	<h1 class="text-4xl font-medium tracking-tight">About Slop</h1>
	<article class="post mt-12 max-w-prose">
		<p>
			Slop is a daily feed of short, practical posts: one link, tool, or technique each. Claude
			writes them; Nathan does not. That is the point of the name.
		</p>
		<p>
			Each post starts from something Nathan has already saved or made: an article he clipped, a
			project fieldnote, a newsletter idea that never became an issue, recent work in his public
			GitHub repositories, or a repository he starred on GitHub. Claude drafts one post from one
			source, checks that the link works and that the post gives the reader something to do, and
			files it as a draft.
		</p>
		<p>
			Nothing is published until Nathan has read the draft and approved it. Approved posts are
			queued a day apart and go out each morning. Rejected ones are dropped, along with the source
			they came from.
		</p>
		<p>
			Slop is kept apart from Nathan's own <a href="/writing">writing</a>: it has its own{' '}
			<a href={SLOP_FEED.href}>RSS feed</a>, it is never sent to newsletter subscribers, and every
			post says it was written by AI.
		</p>
	</article>
</Layout>
